--allman false
--binarygrouping none
--commas always
--comments indent
--decimalgrouping none
--elseposition same-line
--empty void
--exponentcase lowercase
--hexliteralcase uppercase
--indent 4
--indentcase false
--importgrouping alphabetized
--linebreaks lf
--maxwidth none
--octalgrouping none
--operatorfunc spaced
--patternlet hoist
--ranges spaced
--self remove
--semicolons never
--stripunusedargs always
--trimwhitespace always
--wraparguments preserve
--wrapcollections preserve
# SwiftLint's `opening_brace` rule requires an opening brace on the same line as
# its declaration. SwiftFormat's `wrapMultilineStatementBraces` moves it onto its
# own line whenever the condition wraps, so the two gates contradict each other
# and a file can never satisfy both. SwiftLint wins (it matches the Swift API
# Design Guidelines and the rest of this codebase), so the rule is disabled here.
--disable wrapMultilineStatementBraces
# `hoistTry` moves `try` to the start of the enclosing expression, which inside a
# swift-testing macro argument produces code that does not compile:
#   #expect(!(try await port.sessions()).isEmpty)
# becomes
#   #expect(try await !(port.sessions()).isEmpty)
# Every test in this project is a macro invocation, so the rule is off. Bind the
# awaited value to a local first — it reads better in a failure message anyway.
--disable hoistTry
# `preferKeyPath` rewrites `allSatisfy { $0.flag }` as `allSatisfy(\.flag)`. That
# is usually nicer, but `allSatisfy`/`contains`/`filter` are `rethrows`, and
# inside a swift-testing `#expect` expansion the compiler stops being able to
# infer that the closure does not throw — every such assertion then fails with
# "call can throw, but it is not marked with 'try'". Off, for the same reason as
# hoistTry: the whole test suite is macro invocations.
--disable preferKeyPath
--exclude .ffi,Derived,.build,Modules/FeatureTransfer,Modules/FeatureSharing
